A latte or cappuccino at home doesn't need an espresso machine or any syrup. Warm, frothed milk over strong coffee gets you most of the way there, and making it yourself means you choose exactly what goes in it.

This is part three of [Low-Carb Coffee & Milk](/coffee/guides/low-carb-coffee/).

## Ways to froth milk

**Handheld wand frothers** are small battery-powered whisks. They're cheap and easy to store. Warm the milk first, then hold the wand just under the surface until it doubles. The foam is bubblier than a café's, but it's good for the price.

**Electric jug frothers** heat and froth the milk at the same time with the push of a button. They're the easiest, the most consistent, and they make good, fine foam. Most have settings for hot foam, hot milk and cold foam.

**A French press** works too. Warm the milk, pour it into a clean [French press](/coffee/guides/french-press-coffee/), and pump the plunger up and down quickly for 20 to 30 seconds. It makes surprisingly nice foam.

**A steam wand** on an [espresso machine](/coffee/guides/espresso-at-home/) makes the silky, fine "microfoam" you need for latte art. It takes the most practice and the most expensive gear.

James Hoffmann tried several kinds of foamers and steamers side by side, and it's a good watch before you buy one:

