## Which milks froth best

Foam is held up by protein, and its texture comes from fat. So the milks with more protein froth best.

- **Dairy milk** froths well. Skim makes big, stiff foam, and whole milk makes creamier, richer foam.
- **Ultra-filtered milk** has extra protein, so it froths really well, with about half the carbs of regular milk.
- **Soy and pea milk** have plenty of protein and froth well. Soy is one of the easiest plant milks to work with.
- **Oat milk** froths well, especially "barista" versions, but it has more carbs than most people expect.
- **Coconut milk (carton)** is low in protein, so it makes thin, short-lived foam.
- **Cream and half-and-half** are heavy on fat. They froth into something thick and rich, but not light and airy. Cream mixed with some water is worth experimenting with for a lower-carb latte.

There's a full carb comparison in [Unsweetened Milks and Creamers](/coffee/guides/unsweetened-milks-and-creamers/).

## Technique

- **Start cold.** Cold milk gives you more time to froth before it gets too hot.
- **Don't overheat it.** Around 140 to 150°F (60 to 65°C) is ideal, hot but comfortable to touch the pitcher. Milk actually tastes sweetest around there. Much hotter and it tastes flat or scalded, and the foam falls apart.
- **Froth first, then stop.** Get air in early, while the milk is cooler, then just heat and swirl it.
- **Tap and swirl.** Tap the pitcher on the counter to pop big bubbles, then swirl it so the foam and milk stay mixed and glossy.
- **Pour right away.** Foam starts to separate within a minute or two.

## A low-carb latte at home

1. Make a small, strong coffee: a shot of espresso, a strong [AeroPress](/coffee/guides/aeropress-coffee/), a [capsule](/coffee/guides/capsule-coffee-machines/), or a short, strong pour-over.
2. Froth about 3/4 cup of unsweetened soy, pea or ultra-filtered milk.
3. Pour the milk into the coffee, holding back the foam with a spoon, then spoon the foam on top.
4. Add cinnamon, a drop of vanilla extract, or a few [monk fruit](/ingrts/monk-fruit-sweetener/) drops if you want it sweet.

For iced coffee, most electric frothers can make **cold foam**: froth cold milk without heating it, and spoon it over iced coffee or [cold brew](/coffee/guides/cold-brew-coffee/).

